Reviews:
1. String: don't use C-style string in C++. Easy to use than C-style: concatnation, I/O, etc. Memory: how does C++ string support variable length? the string itself may be in stack, but the buffer of the string is in the heap. 
2. Function: make parameter const if the function is not supposed to modify it (say just print).
3. Memory model: stack (local variable; automatic allocate/deallocate during function calls) vs heap (request memory on-the-fly; you have to manage it yourself!). 
4: Pointer: address of memory; be careful: don't use pointer to local variable outside of function.  
5. Array and buffer overflow 


Part two: coding & practice

0. Memory layout practice.
Open the file called ECMem.cpp. It is very simple. The point is getting to know how the memory is allocated and the exact memory layout. 

1. Buffer overflow
First open: ECBufferOverflow.cpp and complete the tasks as listed in comments 

Now open: ECBufferOverflow2.cpp. We will do some more advanced stuff here...

2. Array and pointer

Open ECArrayPointer.cpp. 
First, complete the code for array using index
Second, do the same thing using pointers, not index.

3. Coding: Open ECStringSep.cpp write a function foo that take a parameter string, and separates its characters and returns it.  Eg - foo("xyz" ) -> "x,y,z"


4. Coding: Open ECSwapCase.cpp and finish the ECSwapCase function. You have to take a string input and swap the case of each character. Eg - ECSwapCase("Hello") -> "hELLO"

